{
  id: 'arrays', name: 'Arrays', tagline: 'A row of boxes, each with a number.',
  theory: 'An array (a list) is a row of boxes in a line. Each box has a position number called an index, starting at 0. So the first box is box 0, the second is box 1, and so on.\n\nYou can jump straight to any box if you know its index — that is O(1), super fast. But if you want to find a value and you do not know where it is, you have to walk through the boxes one by one — that is O(n).\n\nMost array problems are about walking the list once, keeping one or two useful numbers as you go (like the biggest so far, or the running total).',
  visual: 'index:   0    1    2    3    4\nvalue:  [10] [20] [30] [40] [50]\n\nnums[0] = 10   <- jump straight here: O(1)\nnums[2] = 30   <- jump straight here: O(1)',
  steps: ['1. Read the question and ask: what do I need to remember while I walk?', '2. Walk the list once, left to right.', '3. Update your remembered values at each box.', '4. The answer is in the remembered values at the end.'],
  pyBasic: 'nums = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]\nprint(nums[0])   # 10 - first box\nprint(nums[-1])  # 50 - last box\nprint(len(nums)) # 5 - how many boxes\n\ntotal = 0\nfor x in nums:\n    total += x\nprint(total)  # 150',
  javaBasic: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\n    System.out.println(nums[0]); // 10\n    System.out.println(nums[nums.length - 1]); // 50\n    System.out.println(nums.length); // 5\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) total += x;\n    System.out.println(total); // 150\n  }\n}',
  pyMedium: 'nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]\n# Maximum Subarray: biggest sum of a run of boxes\nbest = nums[0]\nrunning = 0\nfor x in nums:\n    running += x\n    if running > best:\n        best = running\n    if running < 0:\n        running = 0\nprint(best)  # 6  (from 4, -1, 2, 1)',
  javaMedium: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4};\n    int best = nums[0], running = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n      running += x;\n      if (running > best) best = running;\n      if (running < 0) running = 0;\n    }\n    System.out.println(best); // 6\n  }\n}',
  trace: [['start', 'total = 0', 'nothing added yet'], ['x = 10', 'total = 10', 'add the first box'], ['x = 20', 'total = 30', 'add the second box'], ['x = 30', 'total = 60', 'add the third box'], ['x = 40', 'total = 100', 'add the fourth box'], ['x = 50', 'total = 150', 'add the last box'], ['end', 'answer = 150', 'one walk: O(n)']],
  real: ['A shopping app keeps your cart as a list — adding, removing and totalling items are all array walks.', 'A music app keeps a playlist as a list and jumps straight to song number 5 with its index.'],
  tiny: { t: 'Tiny: add up only the even numbers in a list.', py: 'nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\ntotal = 0\nfor x in nums:\n    if x % 2 == 0:\n        total += x\nprint(total)  # 6', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4};\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) if (x % 2 == 0) total += x;\n    System.out.println(total); // 6\n  }\n}' },
  problems: [
    { t: 'Maximum Subarray: find the biggest sum of boxes in a row.', lc: { n: 53, slug: 'maximum-subarray', title: 'Maximum Subarray' }, py: 'nums = [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]\nbest = nums[0]\nrunning = 0\nfor x in nums:\n    running += x\n    if running > best:\n        best = running\n    if running < 0:\n        running = 0\nprint(best)  # 6', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4};\n    int best = nums[0], running = 0;\n    for (int x : nums) {\n      running += x;\n      if (running > best) best = running;\n      if (running < 0) running = 0;\n    }\n    System.out.println(best); // 6\n  }\n}', hint: 'Keep a running sum. If it goes below 0, throw it away and start fresh — a negative start only makes the sum smaller.' },
    { t: 'Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock: buy low, sell high, one time. What is the biggest profit?', lc: { n: 121, slug: 'best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock', title: 'Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock' }, py: 'prices = [7, 1, 5, 3, 6, 4]\ncheapest = prices[0]\nbest = 0\nfor p in prices:\n    if p < cheapest:\n        cheapest = p\n    if p - cheapest > best:\n        best = p - cheapest\nprint(best)  # 5 (buy at 1, sell at 6)', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] prices = {7, 1, 5, 3, 6, 4};\n    int cheapest = prices[0], best = 0;\n    for (int p : prices) {\n      if (p < cheapest) cheapest = p;\n      if (p - cheapest > best) best = p - cheapest;\n    }\n    System.out.println(best); // 5\n  }\n}', hint: 'Remember the cheapest price seen so far. At each day, the profit if you sold today is price minus cheapest.' },
    { t: 'Product of Array Except Self: for each box, multiply all the other boxes.', lc: { n: 238, slug: 'product-of-array-except-self', title: 'Product of Array Except Self' }, py: 'nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nanswer = [1, 1, 1, 1]\nleft = 1\nfor i in range(4):\n    answer[i] = left\n    left *= nums[i]\nright = 1\nfor i in range(3, -1, -1):\n    answer[i] *= right\n    right *= nums[i]\nprint(answer)  # [24, 12, 8, 6]', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4};\n    int[] answer = {1, 1, 1, 1};\n    int left = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) { answer[i] = left; left *= nums[i]; }\n    int right = 1;\n    for (int i = 3; i >= 0; i--) { answer[i] *= right; right *= nums[i]; }\n    System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(answer)); // [24, 12, 8, 6]\n  }\n}', hint: 'Walk left to right remembering the product of everything before. Then walk right to left remembering the product of everything after. Multiply them.' },
    { t: 'Reverse the list in place, without making a new list.', lc: null, py: 'nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nleft = 0\nright = len(nums) - 1\nwhile left < right:\n    nums[left], nums[right] = nums[right], nums[left]\n    left += 1\n    right -= 1\nprint(nums)  # [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n    int left = 0, right = nums.length - 1;\n    while (left < right) {\n      int temp = nums[left];\n      nums[left] = nums[right];\n      nums[right] = temp;\n      left++; right--;\n    }\n    System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(nums)); // [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]\n  }\n}', hint: 'Use two fingers: one at the start, one at the end. Swap them, then move both towards the middle.' }
  ]
},
{
  id: 'twoptr', name: 'Two Pointers & Sliding Window', tagline: 'Two fingers walking the list together.',
  theory: 'Two pointers means you put one finger at the start of the list and one finger at the end (or both at the start), and you move them towards each other following a simple rule. You never go backwards, so the whole thing is one walk — O(n).\n\nA sliding window is the same idea for a group of boxes in a row: the window is the boxes between your two fingers. You slide it along, adding the new box on the right and dropping the box that falls out on the left. It is how you answer "the biggest sum of 3 boxes in a row" without re-adding everything every time.',
  visual: 'Two pointers (opposite ends):\n  L -> [1] [2] [3] [4] [5] <- R\n  L moves right, R moves left, they meet in the middle.\n\nSliding window of size 3:\n  [1, 2, 3] 4, 5      window = 1+2+3 = 6\n  1, [2, 3, 4] 5      window = 2+3+4 = 9\n  1, 2, [3, 4, 5]    window = 3+4+5 = 12',
  steps: ['1. Put your two fingers where the question tells you (ends, or both at the start).', '2. Check the boxes under your fingers.', '3. Move the finger that the rule tells you to move — only forwards.', '4. Stop when the fingers meet or pass each other.'],
  pyBasic: 'nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n# Is the list a palindrome? (same forwards and backwards)\nleft = 0\nright = len(nums) - 1\nsame = True\nwhile left < right:\n    if nums[left] != nums[right]:\n        same = False\n    left += 1\n    right -= 1\nprint(same)  # True',
  javaBasic: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n    int left = 0, right = nums.length - 1;\n    boolean same = true;\n    while (left < right) {\n      if (nums[left] != nums[right]) same = false;\n      left++; right--;\n    }\n    System.out.println(same); // true\n  }\n}',
  pyMedium: 'nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n# Biggest sum of 3 boxes in a row (sliding window)\nwindow = nums[0] + nums[1] + nums[2]\nbest = window\nfor i in range(3, len(nums)):\n    window = window - nums[i - 3] + nums[i]\n    if window > best:\n        best = window\nprint(best)  # 12',
  javaMedium: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n    int window = nums[0] + nums[1] + nums[2];\n    int best = window;\n    for (int i = 3; i < nums.length; i++) {\n      window = window - nums[i - 3] + nums[i];\n      if (window > best) best = window;\n    }\n    System.out.println(best); // 12\n  }\n}',
  trace: [['start', 'window = 1+2+3 = 6, best = 6', 'first window'], ['i = 3', 'window = 6 - 1 + 4 = 9, best = 9', 'drop 1, add 4'], ['i = 4', 'window = 9 - 2 + 5 = 12, best = 12', 'drop 2, add 5'], ['end', 'answer = 12', 'one walk: O(n)']],
  real: ['Video players use a sliding window to keep the next few seconds ready while dropping what you already watched.', 'A weather app finds the hottest 7 days in a row with a sliding window over daily temperatures.'],
  tiny: { t: 'Tiny: add the first and last number, then the second and second-last. Print the pairs.', py: 'nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nleft = 0\nright = len(nums) - 1\nwhile left < right:\n    print(nums[left] + nums[right])\n    left += 1\n    right -= 1\n# prints 5 then 5', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4};\n    int left = 0, right = nums.length - 1;\n    while (left < right) {\n      System.out.println(nums[left] + nums[right]);\n      left++; right--;\n    }\n  }\n}' },
  problems: [
    { t: 'Two Sum in a sorted list: find two numbers that add to 9. Return their positions.', lc: null, py: 'nums = [2, 7, 11, 15]\ntarget = 9\nleft = 0\nright = len(nums) - 1\nwhile left < right:\n    total = nums[left] + nums[right]\n    if total == target:\n        print(left, right)  # 0 1\n        break\n    if total < target:\n        left += 1\n    else:\n        right -= 1', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {2, 7, 11, 15};\n    int target = 9, left = 0, right = nums.length - 1;\n    while (left < right) {\n      int total = nums[left] + nums[right];\n      if (total == target) { System.out.println(left + " " + right); break; }\n      if (total < target) left++; else right--;\n    }\n  }\n}', hint: 'If the sum is too small, move the left finger right to get a bigger number. If too big, move the right finger left.' },
    { t: 'Remove duplicates from a sorted list, in place.', lc: null, py: 'nums = [1, 1, 2, 2, 3]\nwrite = 1\nfor read in range(1, len(nums)):\n    if nums[read] != nums[read - 1]:\n        nums[write] = nums[read]\n        write += 1\nprint(nums[:write])  # [1, 2, 3]', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {1, 1, 2, 2, 3};\n    int write = 1;\n    for (int read = 1; read < nums.length; read++) {\n      if (nums[read] != nums[read - 1]) { nums[write] = nums[read]; write++; }\n    }\n    System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(java.util.Arrays.copyOf(nums, write))); // [1, 2, 3]\n  }\n}', hint: 'One finger reads every box. The other finger writes only when the box is different from the one before it.' },
    { t: 'Move all zeroes to the end, keeping the other numbers in order.', lc: null, py: 'nums = [0, 1, 0, 3, 12]\nwrite = 0\nfor read in range(len(nums)):\n    if nums[read] != 0:\n        nums[write] = nums[read]\n        write += 1\nfor i in range(write, len(nums)):\n    nums[i] = 0\nprint(nums)  # [1, 3, 12, 0, 0]', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {0, 1, 0, 3, 12};\n    int write = 0;\n    for (int read = 0; read < nums.length; read++)\n      if (nums[read] != 0) nums[write++] = nums[read];\n    for (int i = write; i < nums.length; i++) nums[i] = 0;\n    System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(nums)); // [1, 3, 12, 0, 0]\n  }\n}', hint: 'Same two-finger trick: one finger reads, the other writes only the non-zero numbers. Fill the rest with zeroes.' },
    { t: 'Longest run of the same letter in a word, without a break.', lc: null, py: 'word = "aabbbcc"\nbest = 1\nrun = 1\nfor i in range(1, len(word)):\n    if word[i] == word[i - 1]:\n        run += 1\n    else:\n        run = 1\n    if run > best:\n        best = run\nprint(best)  # 3 (bbb)', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    String word = "aabbbcc";\n    int best = 1, run = 1;\n    for (int i = 1; i < word.length(); i++) {\n      if (word.charAt(i) == word.charAt(i - 1)) run++; else run = 1;\n      if (run > best) best = run;\n    }\n    System.out.println(best); // 3\n  }\n}', hint: 'Walk once. Keep the length of the current run. When the letter changes, the run restarts at 1.' }
  ]
}
