{
  id: 'hashing', name: 'Hashing — The Magic Notebook', tagline: 'Look it up in O(1), like a dictionary.',
  theory: 'A hash map (a dictionary) is like a notebook where you write a word and its meaning next to it. Later you can find the meaning instantly by looking up the word — you never have to read the whole notebook.\n\nIn code, a dictionary stores pairs: a key (the word) and a value (the meaning). Checking "have I seen this number before?" takes one step — O(1) — no matter how big the notebook is. That is why hashing makes so many slow problems fast.\n\nThe trick in most hashing problems: while you walk the list, write each number you have seen into the dictionary. Then every question becomes a quick lookup.',
  visual: 'dictionary = {\n  "apple":  3,     <- key "apple" -> value 3\n  "banana": 1,\n  "mango":  2\n}\n\ndictionary["apple"]  ->  3   (one step, O(1))\n"apple" in dictionary -> True (one step, O(1))',
  steps: ['1. Ask: what do I need to look up quickly later?', '2. Walk the list once, writing things into the dictionary as you go.', '3. Before writing, check the dictionary — the answer is often already there.', '4. Return what you found.'],
  pyBasic: 'marks = {"yash": 90, "megh": 85}\nprint(marks["yash"])  # 90\nmarks["megh"] = 95       # update\nprint("megh" in marks)  # True\n\n# Count letters in a word\ncounts = {}\nfor ch in "banana":\n    counts[ch] = counts.get(ch, 0) + 1\nprint(counts)  # {a: 3, b: 1, n: 2}',
  javaBasic: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Map<String, Integer> marks = new HashMap<>();\n    marks.put("yash", 90);\n    marks.put("megh", 85);\n    System.out.println(marks.get("yash")); // 90\n    marks.put("megh", 95);\n    System.out.println(marks.containsKey("megh")); // true\n    Map<Character, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();\n    for (char ch : "banana".toCharArray())\n      counts.put(ch, counts.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);\n    System.out.println(counts); // {a=3, b=1, n=2}\n  }\n}',
  pyMedium: 'nums = [2, 7, 11, 15]\ntarget = 9\n# Two Sum: find two numbers that add to the target\nseen = {}  # number -> its position\nfor i, x in enumerate(nums):\n    need = target - x\n    if need in seen:\n        print(seen[need], i)  # 0 1\n        break\n    seen[x] = i',
  javaMedium: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {2, 7, 11, 15};\n    int target = 9;\n    Map<Integer, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n      int need = target - nums[i];\n      if (seen.containsKey(need)) {\n        System.out.println(seen.get(need) + " " + i); // 0 1\n        break;\n      }\n      seen.put(nums[i], i);\n    }\n  }\n}',
  trace: [['i = 0, x = 2', 'need = 7, seen = {}', '7 not seen yet. Write 2 -> 0'], ['i = 1, x = 7', 'need = 2, seen = {2: 0}', '2 IS in the notebook at position 0!'], ['end', 'answer = positions 0 and 1', 'one walk: O(n), one lookup each time: O(1)']],
  real: ['A login system stores usernames in a hash map so it can check your password in one step, even with a billion users.', 'Your phone keyboard suggests the next word by looking up what you usually type after the last word — a hash map lookup.'],
  tiny: { t: 'Tiny: find the first letter that appears twice in a word.', py: 'word = "abca"\nseen = set()\nfor ch in word:\n    if ch in seen:\n        print(ch)  # a\n        break\n    seen.add(ch)', java: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    String word = "abca";\n    Set<Character> seen = new HashSet<>();\n    for (char ch : word.toCharArray()) {\n      if (seen.contains(ch)) { System.out.println(ch); break; }\n      seen.add(ch);\n    }\n  }\n}' },
  problems: [
    { t: 'Two Sum: find two numbers that add to 9. Return their positions.', lc: { n: 1, slug: 'two-sum', title: 'Two Sum' }, py: 'nums = [2, 7, 11, 15]\ntarget = 9\nseen = {}\nfor i, x in enumerate(nums):\n    need = target - x\n    if need in seen:\n        print(seen[need], i)  # 0 1\n        break\n    seen[x] = i', java: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {2, 7, 11, 15};\n    int target = 9;\n    Map<Integer, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n      int need = target - nums[i];\n      if (seen.containsKey(need)) { System.out.println(seen.get(need) + " " + i); break; }\n      seen.put(nums[i], i);\n    }\n  }\n}', hint: 'For each number x, ask: "have I seen (target - x) before?" If yes, you found the pair.' },
    { t: 'Has Duplicate? Answer True if any number appears twice.', lc: { n: 217, slug: 'contains-duplicate', title: 'Contains Duplicate' }, py: 'nums = [1, 2, 3, 1]\nseen = set()\nanswer = False\nfor x in nums:\n    if x in seen:\n        answer = True\n    seen.add(x)\nprint(answer)  # True', java: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 1};\n    Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();\n    boolean answer = false;\n    for (int x : nums) { if (seen.contains(x)) answer = true; seen.add(x); }\n    System.out.println(answer); // true\n  }\n}', hint: 'A set is a notebook with no values — just the keys. If you see a number twice, answer True.' },
    { t: 'First letter that appears only once in a word.', lc: null, py: 'word = "aabbc"\ncounts = {}\nfor ch in word:\n    counts[ch] = counts.get(ch, 0) + 1\nfor ch in word:\n    if counts[ch] == 1:\n        print(ch)  # c\n        break', java: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    String word = "aabbc";\n    Map<Character, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();\n    for (char ch : word.toCharArray()) counts.put(ch, counts.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);\n    for (char ch : word.toCharArray())\n      if (counts.get(ch) == 1) { System.out.println(ch); break; } // c\n  }\n}', hint: 'First walk: count every letter. Second walk: the first letter with count 1 is the answer.' },
    { t: 'Group words that are anagrams of each other (same letters, different order).', lc: null, py: 'words = ["eat", "tea", "tan", "ate", "nat", "bat"]\ngroups = {}\nfor w in words:\n    key = "".join(sorted(w))\n    if key not in groups:\n        groups[key] = []\n    groups[key].append(w)\nprint(list(groups.values()))\n# [["eat", "tea", "ate"], ["tan", "nat"], ["bat"]]', java: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    String[] words = {"eat", "tea", "tan", "ate", "nat", "bat"};\n    Map<String, List<String>> groups = new HashMap<>();\n    for (String w : words) {\n      char[] c = w.toCharArray();\n      Arrays.sort(c);\n      String key = new String(c);\n      groups.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(w);\n    }\n    System.out.println(new ArrayList<>(groups.values()));\n  }\n}', hint: 'Anagrams have the same sorted letters. Use the sorted word as the dictionary key — anagrams land in the same group.' }
  ]
},
{
  id: 'recursion', name: 'Recursion — A Function That Calls Itself', tagline: 'Solve a small piece, let the function do the rest.',
  theory: 'Recursion is when a function solves a problem by asking itself to solve a smaller piece of the same problem. Like Russian dolls: each doll contains a smaller doll, until you reach the tiniest doll that cannot open any more.\n\nEvery recursive function needs two things: a base case — the tiniest problem, solved directly, with no more calls — and a recursive step — "my answer = a small step + the answer of the smaller problem".\n\nIf you forget the base case, the function calls itself forever and the program crashes. Always write the base case first.',
  visual: 'factorial(4) = 4 * factorial(3)\n  factorial(3) = 3 * factorial(2)\n    factorial(2) = 2 * factorial(1)\n      factorial(1) = 1        <- base case, stop!\n    = 2 * 1 = 2\n  = 3 * 2 = 6\n= 4 * 6 = 24',
  steps: ['1. Write the base case first — the smallest input, answered directly.', '2. Write the recursive step — how the big answer uses the smaller answer.', '3. Check that every call makes the problem smaller.', '4. Trace it on paper with a tiny number.'],
  pyBasic: 'def factorial(n):\n    if n <= 1:      # base case\n        return 1\n    return n * factorial(n - 1)  # smaller problem\n\nprint(factorial(4))  # 24\n\ndef countdown(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return\n    print(n)\n    countdown(n - 1)\n\ncountdown(3)  # 3, 2, 1',
  javaBasic: 'public class Main {\n  static int factorial(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1; // base case\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);\n  }\n  static void countdown(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return;\n    System.out.println(n);\n    countdown(n - 1);\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(factorial(4)); // 24\n    countdown(3); // 3, 2, 1\n  }\n}',
  pyMedium: 'def fib(n):\n    if n <= 1:  # base case\n        return n\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\n\nprint(fib(6))  # 8  (0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8)\n\n# Faster: remember answers you already found\nmemo = {}\ndef fib_fast(n):\n    if n in memo:\n        return memo[n]\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n    memo[n] = fib_fast(n - 1) + fib_fast(n - 2)\n    return memo[n]\n\nprint(fib_fast(30))  # 832040 - instant!',
  javaMedium: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  static int fib(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return n;\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n  }\n  static Map<Integer, Integer> memo = new HashMap<>();\n  static int fibFast(int n) {\n    if (memo.containsKey(n)) return memo.get(n);\n    if (n <= 1) return n;\n    int v = fibFast(n - 1) + fibFast(n - 2);\n    memo.put(n, v);\n    return v;\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(fib(6)); // 8\n    System.out.println(fibFast(30)); // 832040\n  }\n}',
  trace: [['fib(4)', 'needs fib(3) + fib(2)', 'too big, split it'], ['fib(3)', 'needs fib(2) + fib(1)', 'split again'], ['fib(2)', 'needs fib(1) + fib(0)', 'split again'], ['fib(1) = 1, fib(0) = 0', 'base cases!', 'no more splitting'], ['fib(2) = 1', 'fib(3) = 2', 'answers climb back up'], ['fib(4) = 3', 'answer = 3', 'done']],
  real: ['A file explorer lists folders inside folders inside folders — that is recursion in real life.', 'Google Docs undo history and the "find in page" of nested menus both use recursive walks.'],
  tiny: { t: 'Tiny: add all numbers from 1 to n using recursion.', py: 'def total(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    return n + total(n - 1)\n\nprint(total(5))  # 15', java: 'public class Main {\n  static int total(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 0;\n    return n + total(n - 1);\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(total(5)); // 15\n  }\n}' },
  problems: [
    { t: 'Factorial: 5! = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1. Write it with recursion.', lc: null, py: 'def factorial(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 1\n    return n * factorial(n - 1)\n\nprint(factorial(5))  # 120', java: 'public class Main {\n  static int factorial(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(factorial(5)); // 120\n  }\n}', hint: 'Base case: factorial(1) = 1. Step: factorial(n) = n × factorial(n-1).' },
    { t: 'Fibonacci: the 6th number in 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8...', lc: null, py: 'def fib(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\n\nprint(fib(6))  # 8', java: 'public class Main {\n  static int fib(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return n;\n    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(fib(6)); // 8\n  }\n}', hint: 'Each number is the sum of the two before it. Base cases: fib(0)=0, fib(1)=1.' },
    { t: 'Reverse a word using recursion.', lc: null, py: 'def reverse(s):\n    if len(s) <= 1:\n        return s\n    return reverse(s[1:]) + s[0]\n\nprint(reverse("hello"))  # olleh', java: 'public class Main {\n  static String reverse(String s) {\n    if (s.length() <= 1) return s;\n    return reverse(s.substring(1)) + s.charAt(0);\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(reverse("hello")); // olleh\n  }\n}', hint: 'Take the first letter off, reverse the rest, then put the first letter at the end.' },
    { t: 'Climbing stairs: 1 or 2 steps at a time. How many ways to climb 4 stairs?', lc: null, py: 'def ways(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 1\n    return ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2)\n\nprint(ways(4))  # 5', java: 'public class Main {\n  static int ways(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\n    return ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2);\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(ways(4)); // 5\n  }\n}', hint: 'To reach step n, your last step came from n-1 or n-2. So ways(n) = ways(n-1) + ways(n-2). It is Fibonacci in disguise!' }
  ]
},
{
  id: 'sortsearch', name: 'Sorting & Binary Search', tagline: 'Sort it once, find anything in O(log n).',
  theory: 'A sorted list is a superpower. In a sorted list you can find any number by guessing the middle: if the middle is too big, the answer must be on the left; if too small, on the right. Each guess throws away half the list. That is binary search, and it is O(log n) — even in a list of a million numbers you need only about 20 guesses.\n\nSorting itself costs O(n log n) with a good sort. The idea is simple: keep splitting the list in half, sort the halves, and merge them back together. You will use the built-in sort most of the time — but you must know what it costs.',
  visual: 'Find 7 in [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13]:\n  middle = 7  -> found in 1 guess!\n\nFind 3 in [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13]:\n  middle = 7, too big -> look left [1, 3, 5]\n  middle = 3  -> found in 2 guesses!',
  steps: ['1. Make sure the list is sorted first.', '2. Look at the middle box.', '3. Too big? Throw away the right half. Too small? Throw away the left half.', '4. Repeat on the half that is left.'],
  pyBasic: 'nums = [5, 2, 9, 1]\nnums.sort()\nprint(nums)  # [1, 2, 5, 9]\n\n# Binary search by hand\nnums = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]\ntarget = 7\nleft, right = 0, len(nums) - 1\nwhile left <= right:\n    mid = (left + right) // 2\n    if nums[mid] == target:\n        print(mid)  # 3\n        break\n    if nums[mid] < target:\n        left = mid + 1\n    else:\n        right = mid - 1',
  javaBasic: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {5, 2, 9, 1};\n    Arrays.sort(nums);\n    System.out.println(Arrays.toString(nums)); // [1, 2, 5, 9]\n    int[] sorted = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9};\n    int target = 7, left = 0, right = sorted.length - 1;\n    while (left <= right) {\n      int mid = (left + right) / 2;\n      if (sorted[mid] == target) { System.out.println(mid); break; } // 3\n      if (sorted[mid] < target) left = mid + 1; else right = mid - 1;\n    }\n  }\n}',
  pyMedium: '# Find the first and last position of 8 in a sorted list\nnums = [5, 7, 7, 8, 8, 10]\ntarget = 8\ndef find(nums, target, first):\n    left, right = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    answer = -1\n    while left <= right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        if nums[mid] == target:\n            answer = mid\n            if first:\n                right = mid - 1\n            else:\n                left = mid + 1\n        elif nums[mid] < target:\n            left = mid + 1\n        else:\n            right = mid - 1\n    return answer\nprint(find(nums, 8, True), find(nums, 8, False))  # 3 4',
  javaMedium: 'public class Main {\n  static int find(int[] nums, int target, boolean first) {\n    int left = 0, right = nums.length - 1, answer = -1;\n    while (left <= right) {\n      int mid = (left + right) / 2;\n      if (nums[mid] == target) {\n        answer = mid;\n        if (first) right = mid - 1; else left = mid + 1;\n      } else if (nums[mid] < target) left = mid + 1;\n      else right = mid - 1;\n    }\n    return answer;\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {5, 7, 7, 8, 8, 10};\n    System.out.println(find(nums, 8, true) + " " + find(nums, 8, false)); // 3 4\n  }\n}',
  trace: [['list', '[1, 3, 5, 7, 9], target = 3', 'sorted, ready'], ['mid = 2', 'nums[2] = 5, too big', 'throw away the right half'], ['list now', '[1, 3], target = 3', 'only the left half is left'], ['mid = 0', 'nums[0] = 1, too small', 'throw away the left half'], ['mid = 1', 'nums[1] = 3, found!', 'answer = position 1'], ['end', '3 guesses for 5 numbers', 'a million numbers would need only ~20 guesses']],
  real: ['Your contacts app finds a name in a sorted list with binary search — that is why it feels instant.', 'A dictionary app jumps to the right page by guessing the middle, just like binary search.'],
  tiny: { t: 'Tiny: in a sorted list, is 5 present? Answer True or False.', py: 'nums = [1, 3, 5, 7]\nprint(5 in nums)  # True', java: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {1, 3, 5, 7};\n    System.out.println(Arrays.binarySearch(nums, 5) >= 0); // true\n  }\n}' },
  problems: [
    { t: 'Binary search: find the position of 7 in [1, 3, 5, 7, 9].', lc: null, py: 'nums = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]\ntarget = 7\nleft, right = 0, len(nums) - 1\nwhile left <= right:\n    mid = (left + right) // 2\n    if nums[mid] == target:\n        print(mid)  # 3\n        break\n    if nums[mid] < target:\n        left = mid + 1\n    else:\n        right = mid - 1', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9};\n    int target = 7, left = 0, right = nums.length - 1;\n    while (left <= right) {\n      int mid = (left + right) / 2;\n      if (nums[mid] == target) { System.out.println(mid); break; }\n      if (nums[mid] < target) left = mid + 1; else right = mid - 1;\n    }\n  }\n}', hint: 'Look at the middle. Too big means go left, too small means go right. Half the list disappears each time.' },
    { t: 'Sort these numbers from smallest to biggest, then print the middle one.', lc: null, py: 'nums = [9, 1, 7, 3]\nnums.sort()\nprint(nums)        # [1, 3, 7, 9]\nprint(nums[1])     # 3 (one of the middles)', java: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {9, 1, 7, 3};\n    Arrays.sort(nums);\n    System.out.println(Arrays.toString(nums)); // [1, 3, 7, 9]\n  }\n}', hint: 'The built-in sort does the hard work. Sorting first makes every later question easier.' },
    { t: 'Find the smallest number that is bigger than 6 in a sorted list.', lc: null, py: 'nums = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]\nfor x in nums:\n    if x > 6:\n        print(x)  # 7\n        break', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9};\n    for (int x : nums) if (x > 6) { System.out.println(x); break; } // 7\n  }\n}', hint: 'Walk from the left. The first number bigger than 6 is the answer — the list is sorted, so you can stop early.' },
    { t: 'Square every number, then sort the answers.', lc: null, py: 'nums = [-4, -1, 0, 3, 10]\nsquares = []\nfor x in nums:\n    squares.append(x * x)\nsquares.sort()\nprint(squares)  # [0, 1, 9, 16, 100]', java: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    int[] nums = {-4, -1, 0, 3, 10};\n    int[] squares = new int[nums.length];\n    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) squares[i] = nums[i] * nums[i];\n    Arrays.sort(squares);\n    System.out.println(Arrays.toString(squares)); // [0, 1, 9, 16, 100]\n  }\n}', hint: 'Change each number first, then sort. Two simple steps, one after the other.' }
  ]
}
