{
  id: 'linkedlist', name: 'Linked List — A Chain of Boxes', tagline: 'Each box points to the next one.',
  theory: 'A linked list is a chain: each box (called a node) holds a value and a pointer to the next box. There is no index — to reach the 5th box you must walk through boxes 1, 2, 3 and 4. That is the price you pay.\n\nThe reward: adding a box in the middle is instant. In an array you would have to shift everything; in a chain you just change two pointers. That is why linked lists are used for things that keep changing, like a browser history or a music queue.\n\nThe most common linked-list trick is two pointers walking the chain at different speeds — the fast one and the slow one. If the fast one laps the slow one, there is a loop.',
  visual: 'head\n  |\n  v\n[10] -> [20] -> [30] -> [40] -> None\n  ^                              ^\n  first box                   end of chain\n\nEach box:  value | pointer to next box',
  steps: ['1. Always keep a pointer to the head (the first box).', '2. Walk with a "current" pointer: current = current.next.', '3. To change the chain, change pointers — never move the boxes.', '4. Draw the chain on paper before you code it.'],
  pyBasic: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = None\n\nhead = Node(10)\nhead.next = Node(20)\nhead.next.next = Node(30)\n\n# Walk the chain and print it\ncurrent = head\nwhile current is not None:\n    print(current.value)  # 10, 20, 30\n    current = current.next',
  javaBasic: 'class Node {\n  int value;\n  Node next;\n  Node(int v) { value = v; }\n}\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node head = new Node(10);\n    head.next = new Node(20);\n    head.next.next = new Node(30);\n    Node current = head;\n    while (current != null) {\n      System.out.println(current.value); // 10, 20, 30\n      current = current.next;\n    }\n  }\n}',
  pyMedium: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = None\n\n# Reverse the chain: 10 -> 20 -> 30 becomes 30 -> 20 -> 10\ndef reverse(head):\n    prev = None\n    current = head\n    while current is not None:\n        nxt = current.next\n        current.next = prev\n        prev = current\n        current = nxt\n    return prev\n\nhead = Node(10)\nhead.next = Node(20)\nhead.next.next = Node(30)\nhead = reverse(head)\ncurrent = head\nwhile current:\n    print(current.value)  # 30, 20, 10\n    current = current.next',
  javaMedium: 'class Node {\n  int value; Node next;\n  Node(int v) { value = v; }\n}\npublic class Main {\n  static Node reverse(Node head) {\n    Node prev = null, current = head;\n    while (current != null) {\n      Node nxt = current.next;\n      current.next = prev;\n      prev = current;\n      current = nxt;\n    }\n    return prev;\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node head = new Node(10);\n    head.next = new Node(20);\n    head.next.next = new Node(30);\n    head = reverse(head);\n    for (Node c = head; c != null; c = c.next)\n      System.out.println(c.value); // 30, 20, 10\n  }\n}',
  trace: [['start', 'prev = None, current = 10', 'nothing reversed yet'], ['step 1', 'current = 10, next = 20', 'point 10 backwards to None'], ['step 2', 'current = 20, next = 30', 'point 20 backwards to 10'], ['step 3', 'current = 30, next = None', 'point 30 backwards to 20'], ['end', 'prev = 30, the new head', 'chain is now 30 -> 20 -> 10']],
  real: ['Your browser Back button walks a linked list of pages you visited.', 'A music app keeps the "Up Next" queue as a chain so it can insert a song in the middle instantly.'],
  tiny: { t: 'Tiny: count how many boxes are in the chain.', py: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = None\n\nhead = Node(10)\nhead.next = Node(20)\nhead.next.next = Node(30)\ncount = 0\ncurrent = head\nwhile current is not None:\n    count += 1\n    current = current.next\nprint(count)  # 3', java: 'class Node { int value; Node next; Node(int v) { value = v; } }\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node head = new Node(10);\n    head.next = new Node(20);\n    head.next.next = new Node(30);\n    int count = 0;\n    for (Node c = head; c != null; c = c.next) count++;\n    System.out.println(count); // 3\n  }\n}' },
  problems: [
    { t: 'Print all values in the chain, one per line.', lc: null, py: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = None\n\nhead = Node(10)\nhead.next = Node(20)\nhead.next.next = Node(30)\ncurrent = head\nwhile current is not None:\n    print(current.value)\n    current = current.next', java: 'class Node { int value; Node next; Node(int v) { value = v; } }\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node head = new Node(10);\n    head.next = new Node(20);\n    head.next.next = new Node(30);\n    for (Node c = head; c != null; c = c.next)\n      System.out.println(c.value);\n  }\n}', hint: 'Start at head. Print the value, move to next, repeat until current is None.' },
    { t: 'Find the middle box of the chain.', lc: null, py: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = None\n\nhead = Node(10)\nhead.next = Node(20)\nhead.next.next = Node(30)\nhead.next.next.next = Node(40)\nhead.next.next.next.next = Node(50)\nslow = head\nfast = head\nwhile fast is not None and fast.next is not None:\n    slow = slow.next\n    fast = fast.next.next\nprint(slow.value)  # 30', java: 'class Node { int value; Node next; Node(int v) { value = v; } }\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node head = new Node(10);\n    head.next = new Node(20);\n    head.next.next = new Node(30);\n    head.next.next.next = new Node(40);\n    head.next.next.next.next = new Node(50);\n    Node slow = head, fast = head;\n    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {\n      slow = slow.next;\n      fast = fast.next.next;\n    }\n    System.out.println(slow.value); // 30\n  }\n}', hint: 'Two walkers: slow takes 1 step, fast takes 2. When fast reaches the end, slow is at the middle.' },
    { t: 'Reverse the chain: 10 -> 20 -> 30 becomes 30 -> 20 -> 10.', lc: null, py: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = None\n\ndef reverse(head):\n    prev = None\n    current = head\n    while current is not None:\n        nxt = current.next\n        current.next = prev\n        prev = current\n        current = nxt\n    return prev\n\nhead = Node(10)\nhead.next = Node(20)\nhead.next.next = Node(30)\nhead = reverse(head)\nprint(head.value)  # 30', java: 'class Node { int value; Node next; Node(int v) { value = v; } }\npublic class Main {\n  static Node reverse(Node head) {\n    Node prev = null, current = head;\n    while (current != null) {\n      Node nxt = current.next;\n      current.next = prev;\n      prev = current;\n      current = nxt;\n    }\n    return prev;\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node head = new Node(10);\n    head.next = new Node(20);\n    head.next.next = new Node(30);\n    head = reverse(head);\n    System.out.println(head.value); // 30\n  }\n}', hint: 'Keep three pointers: prev, current, next. At each box, point it backwards, then step forward. Draw it first!' },
    { t: 'Does the chain have a loop? (Does any box point back to an earlier box?)', lc: null, py: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = None\n\nhead = Node(10)\nhead.next = Node(20)\nhead.next.next = Node(30)\nhead.next.next.next = head.next  # loop back to 20\nslow = head\nfast = head\nloop = False\nwhile fast is not None and fast.next is not None:\n    slow = slow.next\n    fast = fast.next.next\n    if slow == fast:\n        loop = True\n        break\nprint(loop)  # True', java: 'class Node { int value; Node next; Node(int v) { value = v; } }\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node head = new Node(10);\n    head.next = new Node(20);\n    head.next.next = new Node(30);\n    head.next.next.next = head.next; // loop\n    Node slow = head, fast = head;\n    boolean loop = false;\n    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {\n      slow = slow.next;\n      fast = fast.next.next;\n      if (slow == fast) { loop = true; break; }\n    }\n    System.out.println(loop); // true\n  }\n}', hint: 'Fast walker takes 2 steps, slow takes 1. If there is a loop, the fast one will lap the slow one and they will meet.' }
  ]
},
{
  id: 'stackqueue', name: 'Stack & Queue — Pile and Line', tagline: 'Stack: last in, first out. Queue: first in, first out.',
  theory: 'A stack is a pile of plates: you put a plate on top, and you take a plate from the top. The last plate you put on is the first one you take off. That is called LIFO — last in, first out.\n\nA queue is a line at a shop: the first person in line is the first person served. That is FIFO — first in, first out.\n\nStacks are perfect for undo buttons and for checking brackets. Queues are perfect for things that must wait their turn, like print jobs or messages.',
  visual: 'STACK (pile of plates)        QUEUE (line at a shop)\n  push 3 -> [3]              front -> [A] [B] [C] <- back\n  push 2 -> [3, 2]           serve A first, then B\n  pop()  -> takes 2 (top!)   new people join at the back',
  steps: ['1. Stack question? Ask: "does the last thing matter most?" (undo, brackets).', '2. Queue question? Ask: "who came first?" (waiting lines, levels).', '3. Push and pop only from the correct end.', '4. Check if it is empty before you pop.'],
  pyBasic: '# Stack: a list, add and remove from the end\nstack = []\nstack.append(1)  # push\nstack.append(2)\nprint(stack.pop())  # 2 - last in, first out\nprint(stack)        # [1]\n\n# Queue: add at the end, remove from the front\nfrom collections import deque\nqueue = deque()\nqueue.append("A")\nqueue.append("B")\nprint(queue.popleft())  # A - first in, first out',
  javaBasic: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    // Stack\n    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    stack.push(1);\n    stack.push(2);\n    System.out.println(stack.pop()); // 2\n    // Queue\n    Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();\n    queue.add("A");\n    queue.add("B");\n    System.out.println(queue.remove()); // A\n  }\n}',
  pyMedium: '# Are the brackets balanced? "([])" is good, "([)]" is bad.\ndef balanced(s):\n    stack = []\n    pairs = {")": "(", "]": "[", "}": "{"}\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch in "([{":\n            stack.append(ch)\n        else:\n            if not stack or stack.pop() != pairs[ch]:\n                return False\n    return len(stack) == 0\n\nprint(balanced("([])"))  # True\nprint(balanced("([)]"))  # False',
  javaMedium: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  static boolean balanced(String s) {\n    Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    Map<Character, Character> pairs = Map.of(\')\', \'(\', \']\', \'[\', \'}\', \'{\');\n    for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {\n      if (ch == \'(\' || ch == \'[\' || ch == \'{\') stack.push(ch);\n      else if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != pairs.get(ch)) return false;\n    }\n    return stack.isEmpty();\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(balanced("([])")); // true\n    System.out.println(balanced("([)]")); // false\n  }\n}',
  trace: [['input', '"([])"', 'read the first bracket'], ['ch = (', 'stack = [(]', 'opening bracket: push it'], ['ch = [', 'stack = [(, []', 'opening bracket: push it'], ['ch = ]', 'stack = [(]', 'closing bracket: pop [ — it matches!'], ['ch = )', 'stack = []', 'closing bracket: pop ( — it matches!'], ['end', 'stack is empty', 'every bracket had a partner: True']],
  real: ['Every code editor checks your brackets with a stack — that is how it knows you forgot a closing bracket.', 'A printer queue holds your print jobs in order — first in, first out.'],
  tiny: { t: 'Tiny: undo a list — push 1, 2, 3, then pop twice. What is left?', py: 'stack = []\nstack.append(1)\nstack.append(2)\nstack.append(3)\nstack.pop()\nstack.pop()\nprint(stack)  # [1]', java: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    stack.push(1); stack.push(2); stack.push(3);\n    stack.pop(); stack.pop();\n    System.out.println(stack); // [1]\n  }\n}' },
  problems: [
    { t: 'Check if brackets are balanced: is "([])" good and "([)]" bad?', lc: null, py: 'def balanced(s):\n    stack = []\n    pairs = {")": "(", "]": "[", "}": "{"}\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch in "([{":\n            stack.append(ch)\n        else:\n            if not stack or stack.pop() != pairs[ch]:\n                return False\n    return len(stack) == 0\n\nprint(balanced("([])"))  # True\nprint(balanced("([)]"))  # False', java: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  static boolean balanced(String s) {\n    Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    Map<Character, Character> pairs = Map.of(\')\', \'(\', \']\', \'[\', \'}\', \'{\');\n    for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {\n      if (ch == \'(\' || ch == \'[\' || ch == \'{\') stack.push(ch);\n      else if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != pairs.get(ch)) return false;\n    }\n    return stack.isEmpty();\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(balanced("([])")); // true\n    System.out.println(balanced("([)]")); // false\n  }\n}', hint: 'Opening bracket? Push it. Closing bracket? Pop and check it matches. At the end the stack must be empty.' },
    { t: 'Reverse a word using a stack.', lc: null, py: 'word = "hello"\nstack = []\nfor ch in word:\n    stack.append(ch)\nanswer = ""\nwhile stack:\n    answer += stack.pop()\nprint(answer)  # olleh', java: 'public class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    String word = "hello";\n    java.util.Deque<Character> stack = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();\n    for (char ch : word.toCharArray()) stack.push(ch);\n    String answer = "";\n    while (!stack.isEmpty()) answer += stack.pop();\n    System.out.println(answer); // olleh\n  }\n}', hint: 'Push all the letters, then pop them — they come out backwards.' },
    { t: 'Serve a line of people in order: A, B, C arrive. Who is served first?', lc: null, py: 'from collections import deque\nqueue = deque()\nqueue.append("A")\nqueue.append("B")\nqueue.append("C")\nprint(queue.popleft())  # A\nprint(queue.popleft())  # B', java: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();\n    queue.add("A"); queue.add("B"); queue.add("C");\n    System.out.println(queue.remove()); // A\n    System.out.println(queue.remove()); // B\n  }\n}', hint: 'A queue serves first-in-first-out. New people join at the back.' },
    { t: 'Min Stack: a stack that can also tell you the smallest number in it, in O(1).', lc: null, py: 'class MinStack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.stack = []\n        self.mins = []\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.stack.append(x)\n        if not self.mins or x <= self.mins[-1]:\n            self.mins.append(x)\n    def pop(self):\n        x = self.stack.pop()\n        if x == self.mins[-1]:\n            self.mins.pop()\n    def get_min(self):\n        return self.mins[-1]\n\ns = MinStack()\ns.push(5); s.push(2); s.push(7)\nprint(s.get_min())  # 2', java: 'import java.util.*;\npublic class Main {\n  static class MinStack {\n    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    Deque<Integer> mins = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    void push(int x) {\n      stack.push(x);\n      if (mins.isEmpty() || x <= mins.peek()) mins.push(x);\n    }\n    void pop() { int x = stack.pop(); if (x == mins.peek()) mins.pop(); }\n    int getMin() { return mins.peek(); }\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    MinStack s = new MinStack();\n    s.push(5); s.push(2); s.push(7);\n    System.out.println(s.getMin()); // 2\n  }\n}', hint: 'Keep a second stack of minimums. Push a number onto it only when it is smaller than (or equal to) the current minimum.' }
  ]
},
{
  id: 'trees', name: 'Trees — Branches Like a Family Tree', tagline: 'One root, branches, leaves. Walk it with recursion.',
  theory: 'A tree is a box (called a node) with branches to child boxes. The top box is the root. Boxes with no children are leaves. Every box has exactly one parent, and there are no loops.\n\nTrees are everywhere: folders on your computer, the "family tree" of comments on a post, the menu of a website.\n\nYou walk a tree with recursion: visit the box, then visit its left child, then its right child. The three famous walks are in-order (left, me, right), pre-order (me, left, right) and post-order (left, right, me). For a binary search tree, in-order gives you the numbers in sorted order — like magic.',
  visual: '        10\n       /  \\\n      5    15\n     / \\     \\\n    2   7    20\n\nin-order walk (left, me, right): 2, 5, 7, 10, 15, 20  <- sorted!',
  steps: ['1. Base case: if the box is empty (None), stop and return.', '2. Do the work for this box.', '3. Recurse into the left child, then the right child.', '4. For a search tree: go left if the target is smaller, right if bigger.'],
  pyBasic: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\nroot = Node(10)\nroot.left = Node(5)\nroot.right = Node(15)\n\n# In-order walk: left, me, right\ndef inorder(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return\n    inorder(node.left)\n    print(node.value)\n    inorder(node.right)\n\ninorder(root)  # 5, 10, 15',
  javaBasic: 'class Node {\n  int value; Node left, right;\n  Node(int v) { value = v; }\n}\npublic class Main {\n  static void inorder(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return;\n    inorder(node.left);\n    System.out.println(node.value);\n    inorder(node.right);\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node root = new Node(10);\n    root.left = new Node(5);\n    root.right = new Node(15);\n    inorder(root); // 5, 10, 15\n  }\n}',
  pyMedium: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\n# How deep is the tree? (longest chain from root to a leaf)\ndef max_depth(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return 0\n    return 1 + max(max_depth(node.left), max_depth(node.right))\n\nroot = Node(10)\nroot.left = Node(5)\nroot.right = Node(15)\nroot.left.left = Node(2)\nprint(max_depth(root))  # 3',
  javaMedium: 'class Node {\n  int value; Node left, right;\n  Node(int v) { value = v; }\n}\npublic class Main {\n  static int maxDepth(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return 0;\n    return 1 + Math.max(maxDepth(node.left), maxDepth(node.right));\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node root = new Node(10);\n    root.left = new Node(5);\n    root.right = new Node(15);\n    root.left.left = new Node(2);\n    System.out.println(maxDepth(root)); // 3\n  }\n}',
  trace: [['inorder(10)', 'go left first', 'visit the left child'], ['inorder(5)', 'go left first', 'visit the left child'], ['inorder(None)', 'empty box: stop', 'base case, return'], ['print 5', 'left done, now me', '5 is printed'], ['inorder(None)', 'empty box: stop', 'right child of 5 is empty'], ['print 10', 'left done, now me', '10 is printed'], ['inorder(15)', 'print 15', 'right side done'], ['end', 'order: 5, 10, 15', 'sorted!']],
  real: ['Your computer folders are a tree: folders inside folders inside folders.', 'A family tree app, a company org chart and a website menu are all trees.'],
  tiny: { t: 'Tiny: count the leaves (boxes with no children) in a small tree.', py: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\ndef count_leaves(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return 0\n    if node.left is None and node.right is None:\n        return 1\n    return count_leaves(node.left) + count_leaves(node.right)\n\nroot = Node(10)\nroot.left = Node(5)\nroot.right = Node(15)\nprint(count_leaves(root))  # 2', java: 'class Node { int value; Node left, right; Node(int v) { value = v; } }\npublic class Main {\n  static int countLeaves(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return 0;\n    if (node.left == null && node.right == null) return 1;\n    return countLeaves(node.left) + countLeaves(node.right);\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node root = new Node(10);\n    root.left = new Node(5);\n    root.right = new Node(15);\n    System.out.println(countLeaves(root)); // 2\n  }\n}' },
  problems: [
    { t: 'Walk the tree in-order and print the numbers (they come out sorted).', lc: null, py: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\ndef inorder(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return\n    inorder(node.left)\n    print(node.value)\n    inorder(node.right)\n\nroot = Node(10)\nroot.left = Node(5)\nroot.right = Node(15)\ninorder(root)  # 5, 10, 15', java: 'class Node { int value; Node left, right; Node(int v) { value = v; } }\npublic class Main {\n  static void inorder(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return;\n    inorder(node.left);\n    System.out.println(node.value);\n    inorder(node.right);\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node root = new Node(10);\n    root.left = new Node(5);\n    root.right = new Node(15);\n    inorder(root); // 5, 10, 15\n  }\n}', hint: 'In-order = left child, then me, then right child. On a search tree this prints sorted numbers.' },
    { t: 'How deep is the tree? Count the longest chain from root to leaf.', lc: null, py: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\ndef max_depth(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return 0\n    return 1 + max(max_depth(node.left), max_depth(node.right))\n\nroot = Node(10)\nroot.left = Node(5)\nroot.right = Node(15)\nroot.left.left = Node(2)\nprint(max_depth(root))  # 3', java: 'class Node { int value; Node left, right; Node(int v) { value = v; } }\npublic class Main {\n  static int maxDepth(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return 0;\n    return 1 + Math.max(maxDepth(node.left), maxDepth(node.right));\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node root = new Node(10);\n    root.left = new Node(5);\n    root.right = new Node(15);\n    root.left.left = new Node(2);\n    System.out.println(maxDepth(root)); // 3\n  }\n}', hint: 'The depth of a box = 1 + the deeper of its two children. An empty box has depth 0.' },
    { t: 'Search for 7 in a binary search tree.', lc: null, py: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\ndef search(node, target):\n    if node is None:\n        return False\n    if node.value == target:\n        return True\n    if target < node.value:\n        return search(node.left, target)\n    return search(node.right, target)\n\nroot = Node(10)\nroot.left = Node(5)\nroot.right = Node(15)\nroot.left.right = Node(7)\nprint(search(root, 7))   # True\nprint(search(root, 99))  # False', java: 'class Node { int value; Node left, right; Node(int v) { value = v; } }\npublic class Main {\n  static boolean search(Node node, int target) {\n    if (node == null) return false;\n    if (node.value == target) return true;\n    if (target < node.value) return search(node.left, target);\n    return search(node.right, target);\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node root = new Node(10);\n    root.left = new Node(5);\n    root.right = new Node(15);\n    root.left.right = new Node(7);\n    System.out.println(search(root, 7)); // true\n    System.out.println(search(root, 99)); // false\n  }\n}', hint: 'Smaller than this box? Go left. Bigger? Go right. You throw away half the tree each time.' },
    { t: 'Add up all the numbers in the tree.', lc: null, py: 'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\ndef total(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return 0\n    return node.value + total(node.left) + total(node.right)\n\nroot = Node(10)\nroot.left = Node(5)\nroot.right = Node(15)\nprint(total(root))  # 30', java: 'class Node { int value; Node left, right; Node(int v) { value = v; } }\npublic class Main {\n  static int total(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return 0;\n    return node.value + total(node.left) + total(node.right);\n  }\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    Node root = new Node(10);\n    root.left = new Node(5);\n    root.right = new Node(15);\n    System.out.println(total(root)); // 30\n  }\n}', hint: 'My total = my value + left total + right total. An empty box contributes 0.' }
  ]
}
